tel 1.0

base distillate
import soundness

info   typed conversions out of text and other forms: `as[T]`, the `As` extractor and their typeclasses
detail
    _Distillate_ turns loose values — command-line arguments, environment variables, regular
    expression captures, a field of a parsed document — into typed ones, and makes the way each
    conversion can fail visible in its type. It does so with three typeclasses, each with its
    own failure mode:

    - `Decodable` reads a value _out of_ a form (`Int is Decodable in Text`) and raises a typed
      error, through a `Tactic`, when it cannot;
    - `Extractable` converts _toward_ a result (`Text is Extractable to Int`) and yields `Unset`
      when it declines, which is what a pattern match wants;
    - `Irrefutable` converts where nothing can go wrong (`Byte is Irrefutable to Int`).

    The everyday surface is two names: the `as` extension, which decodes, and the `As`
    extractor, which extracts in a pattern.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

    t"123".as[Int]                    // 123
    safely(t"hello".as[Int]).or(0)    // 0

    t"12:13:14" match
      case r"${As[Int](h)}(\d+):${As[Int](m)}(\d+):${As[Int](s)}(\d+)" => List(h, m, s)
      case _                                                            => List()
    ```

    The library also carries the pieces every data format shares: `Enumerable`, the derived
    description of an `enum` that every format's codecs use to name its cases; `Identifiable`,
    a two-way mapping between naming conventions; `Parsable`, the streaming counterpart of
    `Decodable` which reads a value straight from a format's token reader; the three
    `Decodable.Absence`, `Decodable.Nullity` and `Decodable.Fault` policies an `Optional` field
    consults; and `Codecs`, the contextual mode that selects between a format's derived and
    direct (generated) codecs. The encoding direction, `Encodable` and `value.in[Format]`,
    belongs to `anticipation`, beneath this library.

    Two choice packages hold givens which must be imported by name, because `import pkg.*`
    does not import givens: `truths`, whose `Truth` spellings are accepted by the `Boolean`
    decoder beside `true`/`false`, and `codecs`, whose three modes select the codec path for
    every format at once.

    The library is a single source set, `distillate.core`, for every platform. Everything is
    exported to the `soundness` umbrella, and the `truths` and `codecs` packages merge with the
    umbrella's own.

refer distillate.Decodable
refer distillate.Extractable
refer distillate.Irrefutable
refer distillate.As
refer distillate.as

entry .as   decodes a value as `T` with the `T is Decodable in` its form, or converts it with a given `Irrefutable`
    `value.as[T]` summons a `T is Decodable in F`, where `F` is the static type of `value`, and
    applies it. A decoder built from a `Tactic` (the number decoders need a
    `Tactic[Number.Error]`, the `Boolean` one a `Tactic[Truth.Error]`) raises through it, so the
    strategy in scope decides whether a failed decode throws, yields `Unset` under `safely`, or
    accrues. Nothing of the decoder is retained: the result is a plain value.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    t"123".as[Int]                          // 123
    capture[Number.Error](t"300".as[Byte])  // Number.Error(..., Reason.OutOfRange)
    ```

    The second overload, `value.as[T](irrefutable)`, takes an `Irrefutable` explicitly, as an
    ordinary argument rather than a contextual one, and applies it; it cannot fail.

entry .As   the extractor `As[T]`, which matches a scrutinee that yields a `T` and binds it
  detail
      `As[T](binding)` in a pattern summons `scrutinee.type is Extractable to T` and matches
      when it yields a value, falling through to the next case when it yields `Unset`. Because
      it is an ordinary extractor it nests in any pattern: in the groups of an `r"…"` pattern,
      inside a list pattern, or beside other extractors.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      List(t"12", t"13", t"14") match
        case As[Int](first) :: As[Int](second) :: As[Int](third) :: Nil => (first, second, third)
        case _                                                          => (0, 0, 0)

      300 match
        case As[Byte](byte) => byte
        case _              => -1      // 300 does not fit in a Byte
      ```

      Under capture checking, the explicit type argument of `As[T]` forces the scrutinee
      through a singleton type which the checker cannot bind to the instance's `Self`; the
      result-fixed extractors `As.Int` and `As.Boolean` take no type argument and are usable
      from capture-checked code.
  refer distillate.Extractable
  entry .unapply   matches a scrutinee whose singleton type is `Extractable` to the result
  entry .Int   an extractor fixed to `Int`, with no type argument, for capture-checked code
    detail
        `case As.Int(number) =>` matches any scrutinee with a `scrutinee is Extractable to Int`
        in scope, inferring the scrutinee's type from the matched value rather than from a
        singleton, so the instance resolves cleanly under capture checking.
    entry .unapply   matches a scrutinee which is `Extractable to Int`
  entry .Boolean   an extractor fixed to `Boolean`, with no type argument, for capture-checked code
    detail
        `case As.Boolean(flag) =>` matches any scrutinee with a `scrutinee is Extractable to
        Boolean` in scope; for text that is exactly `true` or `false`.
    entry .unapply   matches a scrutinee which is `Extractable to Boolean`

entry .Decodable   a typeclass reading `Self` out of a `Form`, raising a typed error when it cannot
  detail
      `T is Decodable in F` is a single function `F => T`, `#decoded`. It is what `value.as[T]`
      applies, and what every data format's codecs build on: `Person is Decodable in Json`,
      `Int is Decodable in Text`. An instance which can fail takes a `Tactic` for its error as
      a contextual parameter and raises through it, so the error is part of what the given
      needs, and the strategy is chosen where `as` is called.

      Writing an instance is writing the function; in a companion object it is found with no
      import.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      enum Heading:
        case N, S, E, W

      object Heading:
        given (Heading is Decodable in Text) =
          case t"north" => N
          case t"south" => S
          case t"east"  => E
          case t"west"  => W

      t"north".as[Heading]   // Heading.N
      ```

      The companion holds the decoders out of `Text` for the primitive types and for any
      `enum`, and the three policies an `Optional` field consults (`.Absence`, `.Nullity`,
      `.Fault`). It extends `Decodable2`, whose lower-priority `generic` decodes any value in
      its own form as itself.

      A format's catch-all given (`[value] => value is Decodable in Json` and the like) ties
      with any companion given over a bare type parameter, which is an ambiguity error; a given
      whose type parameter is bounded (`[item <: Item]`) is more specific and wins.
  refer distillate.as
  refer distillate.Extractable
  refer distillate.Parsable
  refer anticipation.Encodable
  entry .int   decodes an `Int` from decimal text, raising `Number.Error` if it is unparseable
      Needs a `Tactic[Number.Error]`. Parses with `Integer.parseInt`, so the text must be an
      optionally-signed decimal integer with no surrounding space; any failure, including
      overflow, is `Number.Error.Reason.Unparseable`.
  entry .byte   decodes a `Byte` from decimal text, distinguishing unparseable from out-of-range
      Needs a `Tactic[Number.Error]`. Text which is not an integer raises `Number.Error` with
      `Reason.Unparseable`; an integer outside −128 to 127 raises it with `Reason.OutOfRange`.
  entry .short   decodes a `Short` from decimal text, distinguishing unparseable from out-of-range
      Needs a `Tactic[Number.Error]`. Text which is not an integer raises `Number.Error` with
      `Reason.Unparseable`; an integer outside the range of a `Short` raises it with
      `Reason.OutOfRange`.
  entry .long   decodes a `Long` from decimal text, raising `Number.Error` if it is unparseable
      Needs a `Tactic[Number.Error]`; parses with `java.lang.Long.parseLong`.
  entry .double   decodes a `Double` from text, raising `Number.Error` if it is unparseable
      Needs a `Tactic[Number.Error]`; parses with `java.lang.Double.parseDouble`, so exponents,
      `NaN` and `Infinity` are accepted as Java accepts them.
  entry .float   decodes a `Float` from text, raising `Number.Error` if it is unparseable
      Needs a `Tactic[Number.Error]`; parses with `java.lang.Float.parseFloat`.
  entry .char   decodes a `Char` as the first character of the text
      Needs no tactic. Any text beyond the first character is ignored, and empty text is not
      checked for: prefer `As[Char]`, which matches only text of exactly one character.
  entry .boolean   decodes a `Boolean` from any `Truth` spelling in scope, in any case, raising `Truth.Error`
      Needs a `Tactic[Truth.Error]` and collects every `Truth` in scope (`Every[Truth]`). The
      text is trimmed and lowercased, then offered to each spelling in turn; text which none
      recognizes raises `Truth.Error`. `Truth.trueFalse`, in the `Truth` companion, is always
      in scope, and each `truths` given imported adds a spelling beside it rather than
      replacing it.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label Only true and false
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            t" FALSE ".as[Boolean]           // false
            safely(t"yes".as[Boolean])       // Unset: a Truth.Error was raised
      frame
        label Adding yes and no
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import truths.yesNoTruth

            t"yes".as[Boolean]               // true
            t"true".as[Boolean]              // true, still
      frame
        label Adding on/off and 1/0 as well
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import truths.{yesNoTruth, onOffTruth, oneZeroTruth}

            List(t"on", t"OFF", t"1", t"no").map(_.as[Boolean])   // List(true, false, true, false)
      ##
      ```
  entry .enumeration   decodes an `enum` case from its name, raising `Enumerable.Error` if it names none
      For any `enum` with an `Enumerable` (derived automatically) and an `Identifiable` in
      scope, and a `Tactic[Enumerable.Error]`. The text is first mapped through the
      `Identifiable`'s `#decode`, so a naming convention (snake case in, camel case out) can sit
      between the text and the case names; the error lists the valid names, encoded back
      through the same `Identifiable`.
  entry .Absence   the policy for a key the document omits: lenient yields `Unset`, strict raises
    detail
        A policy indexed by its format (`Decodable.Absence in Json`), consulted by every format
        which decodes an `Optional` or `Option` field. The default, `.default`, is lenient for
        every format: an omitted key reads as `Unset` (or `None`). A format's
        `optionalityOptions` package offers a strict given named for the format
        (`strictJsonAbsence`), imported by name, which outranks the default; one may also be
        declared locally.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        summon[Decodable.Absence in Text].strict   // false

        locally:
          given Decodable.Absence in Text = Decodable.Absence(true)
          summon[Decodable.Absence in Text].strict // true
        ```
    entry .apply   an absence policy for a form, strict or lenient
    entry .default   the companion default for every form: lenient
    entry #strict   whether an omitted key is an error rather than `Unset`
  entry .Nullity   the policy for a format's explicit null: lenient yields `Unset`, strict raises
    detail
        A policy indexed by its format (`Decodable.Nullity in Json`), consulted only by formats
        which have a null literal. The default, `.default`, is lenient: a null reads as `Unset`
        (or `None`). A format's `optionalityOptions` package offers a strict given named for the
        format (`strictCborNulls`), imported by name, which outranks the default.
    entry .apply   a nullity policy for a form, strict or lenient
    entry .default   the companion default for every form: lenient
    entry #strict   whether an explicit null is an error rather than `Unset`
  entry .Fault   the policy for a present value the inner decoder rejects: strict raises, lenient yields `Unset`
    detail
        A policy indexed by its format (`Decodable.Fault in Json`). The default, `.default`, is
        strict: the inner decoder's error passes through. A format's `optionalityOptions`
        package offers a lenient given named for the format (`lenientYamlFaults`), imported by
        name; under it a rejected value decodes under the ambient tactic's `tolerate`, so
        nothing accrues under an accumulating tactic either, and reads as `Unset` (or `None`).
    entry .apply   a fault policy for a form, strict or lenient
    entry .default   the companion default for every form: strict
    entry #strict   whether a rejected value raises its error rather than reading as `Unset`
  entry #decodable   this instance, as a stable reference for use within a lambda
  entry #decoded   decodes a value of the form as `Self`
      The single abstract method: a lambda of type `Form => Self` is an instance. It may
      raise through a tactic the instance captured when it was resolved.
  entry #position   enriches a positional focus with what is known of the decoded form
      Identity by default. A format whose decoders track where in a document a value was found
      (Jacinta's `Json.Decodable`) overrides it to refine a focus with information derived
      from the `Form` after decoding.
  entry #map   a decoder in the same form for a new type, applying a function after decoding
      `summon[Int is Decodable in Text].map(Percent(_))` decodes an `Int` and wraps it. The
      result captures both this decoder and the function.

entry .Decodable2   the lower-priority givens of the `Decodable` companion
  detail
      A trait the `Decodable` companion extends, so that its `generic` given ranks below every
      given declared in the companion itself.
  entry #generic   decodes any value in its own form as itself: `T is Decodable in T`
      The identity decoder, so `json.as[Json]` or `text.as[Text]` needs no instance of its own.
      Being in a parent of the companion, it ranks below the companion's specific givens.

entry .Extractable   a typeclass converting `Self` toward a `Result`, yielding `Unset` when it declines
  detail
      `Text is Extractable to Int` is a single function `Text => Optional[Int]`, `#extract`; its
      `#unapply` is what the `As[T]` extractor calls, so an instance is a pattern. It never
      raises: input it cannot convert yields `Unset`, and the match falls through.

      The companion holds conversions from text to each primitive type, every narrowing between
      the numeric types (each declining when the value does not survive the round trip), the
      conversion from text to an `enum` case by name and from an `Int` to one by ordinal, a
      lift through `Optional`, and a bridge from any `Decodable in Text` which runs the decoder
      under `safely`. That bridge is why `As[EmailAddress]` or `As[Uuid]` work for every type
      that can be decoded from text.

      An instance can borrow an existing one:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Percent(value: Int)

      given (Text is Extractable to Int) => Text is Extractable to Percent =
        case As[Int](number) if number >= 0 && number <= 100 => Percent(number)
        case _                                               => Unset

      t"42" match
        case As[Percent](percent) => percent
        case _                    => Percent(0)
      ```
  refer distillate.As
  refer distillate.Decodable
  refer distillate.Irrefutable
  entry .decodable   extracts any type with a `Decodable in Text`, yielding `Unset` where the decoder raises
      The bridge from `Decodable` to `Extractable`: the decoder is applied under `safely`, so
      any error it raises becomes `Unset`. It makes every text-decodable type usable with
      `As[T]`.
  entry .optional   lifts an extractor through `Optional`: `Unset` stays `Unset`
  entry .irrefutable   a `String` converts with the `Irrefutable` of `Text`, after conversion to `Text`
  entry .textChar   extracts a `Char` from text of exactly one character
  entry .textByte   extracts a `Byte` from text, declining text which is unparseable or out of range
  entry .textShort   extracts a `Short` from text, declining text which is unparseable or out of range
  entry .textInt   extracts an `Int` from text, declining text which is not a decimal integer that fits
  entry .textLong   extracts a `Long` from text, declining text which is not a decimal integer that fits
  entry .textFloat   extracts a `Float` from text, declining text which is not a number
  entry .textDouble   extracts a `Double` from text, declining text which is not a number
  entry .textBoolean   extracts a `Boolean` from exactly `true` or `false`, case-sensitively
      Unlike the `Boolean` decoder, which trims, lowercases and consults every `Truth` in scope,
      `As[Boolean]` matches only the two exact words.
  entry .shortByte   narrows a `Short` to a `Byte`, declining a value which does not fit
  entry .intByte   narrows an `Int` to a `Byte`, declining a value which does not fit
  entry .intShort   narrows an `Int` to a `Short`, declining a value which does not fit
  entry .intFloat   converts an `Int` to a `Float`, declining a value which a `Float` cannot hold exactly
  entry .longByte   narrows a `Long` to a `Byte`, declining a value which does not fit
  entry .longShort   narrows a `Long` to a `Short`, declining a value which does not fit
  entry .longInt   narrows a `Long` to an `Int`, declining a value which does not fit
  entry .longFloat   converts a `Long` to a `Float`, declining a value which a `Float` cannot hold exactly
  entry .longDouble   converts a `Long` to a `Double`, declining a value which a `Double` cannot hold exactly
  entry .floatByte   converts a `Float` to a `Byte`, declining a value which is not a whole number that fits
  entry .floatShort   converts a `Float` to a `Short`, declining a value which is not a whole number that fits
  entry .floatInt   converts a `Float` to an `Int`, declining a value which is not a whole number that fits
  entry .floatLong   converts a `Float` to a `Long`, declining a value which is not a whole number that fits
  entry .doubleByte   converts a `Double` to a `Byte`, declining a value which is not a whole number that fits
  entry .doubleShort   converts a `Double` to a `Short`, declining a value which is not a whole number that fits
  entry .doubleInt   converts a `Double` to an `Int`, declining a value which is not a whole number that fits
  entry .doubleLong   converts a `Double` to a `Long`, declining a value which is not a whole number that fits
  entry .doubleFloat   converts a `Double` to a `Float`, declining a value which a `Float` cannot hold exactly
  entry .valueOf   extracts an `enum` case from text naming it exactly, through the enum's `valueOf`
      Needs only the enum's `Mirror`, which the compiler provides: `As[Direction]` matches
      `t"North"` with no instance written. The name must match the case's name exactly; a
      parameterized case, which has no `valueOf`, never matches.
  entry .fromOrdinal   extracts an `enum` case from an `Int`, by its ordinal
      Declines an ordinal outside the enum's range.
  entry #extract   converts a value, or yields `Unset` if it cannot
  entry #unapply   converts a value as an `Option`, for use as an extractor

entry .Irrefutable   a typeclass converting `Self` to a `Result` in a way that cannot fail
  detail
      `Byte is Irrefutable to Int` is a single function `Byte => Int`, `#unapply`; being an
      `unapply` with no `Option`, an instance is an irrefutable pattern. The companion holds
      the conversions between `String` and `Text`, the identity, and every widening between the
      numeric types. It is a `Typeclass.Pure`: an instance captures nothing.

      `value.as[T](irrefutable)` applies one explicitly.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val wide: Long = 42.toByte.as[Long](summon[Byte is Irrefutable to Long])
      ```
  refer distillate.Extractable
  refer distillate.as
  entry .stringText   converts a `String` to `Text`
  entry .irrefutable   a `String` converts with the `Irrefutable` of `Text`, after conversion to `Text`
  entry .textString   converts `Text` to a `String`
  entry .ident   converts any value to itself
  entry .byteShort   widens a `Byte` to a `Short`
  entry .byteInt   widens a `Byte` to an `Int`
  entry .byteLong   widens a `Byte` to a `Long`
  entry .byteFloat   widens a `Byte` to a `Float`
  entry .byteDouble   widens a `Byte` to a `Double`
  entry .shortInt   widens a `Short` to an `Int`
  entry .shortLong   widens a `Short` to a `Long`
  entry .shortFloat   widens a `Short` to a `Float`
  entry .shortDouble   widens a `Short` to a `Double`
  entry .intLong   widens an `Int` to a `Long`
  entry .intDoule   widens an `Int` to a `Double` (the name is misspelt in the source)
  entry .floatDouble   widens a `Float` to a `Double`
  entry #unapply   converts a value; it cannot fail
  entry #contramap   an irrefutable conversion from a new type, applying a pure function first

entry .Enumerable   the derived description of an `enum`: its name, its cases, and lookup by name and ordinal
  detail
      An `Enumerable` is derived for any `enum` whose cases are all singletons, with no
      instance to write: `.derived` reads the cases from the companion's `values`. It gives the
      enumeration's `#name`, its `#values` in declaration order, and lookup in both directions,
      each returning `Optional` rather than raising, since neither a name nor an ordinal is
      guaranteed to correspond to a case. Every format's derived codecs use it to name an
      enum's cases, so they all agree.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      enum Direction:
        case North, South, East, West

      val enumerable = summon[Direction is Enumerable]

      enumerable.values                  // every case, in order
      enumerable.value(t"North")         // Direction.North
      enumerable.value(Prim)             // Direction.North, by ordinal
      enumerable.name(Direction.South)   // t"South"
      enumerable.index(Direction.East)   // 2
      ```

      Derivation halts with _enum `T` is not a simple choice_ for an enum with no `values`
      method, which is one with a parameterized case.
  refer distillate.Decodable.enumeration
  refer distillate.Identifiable
  entry .derived   derives an `Enumerable` for an `enum` of singleton cases, by macro
  entry .Error   text decoded as an enum which names none of its cases
    detail
        Error 900: _the specified variant (`inputLabel`) is not one of the valid variants
        (`validVariants`) of the enumeration `sum`_. Raised by the `Decodable.enumeration`
        decoder.
    entry #inputLabel   the text which named no case
    entry #sum   the enumeration's name
    entry #validVariants   the names of every case, as the decoder would accept them
  entry #name   the enumeration's name, or the name of one of its cases
      Without an argument, the name of the enumeration type as the compiler shows it; applied
      to a case, that case's name.
  entry #values   every case of the enumeration, in declaration order
  entry #value   the case with a given name, or at a given `Ordinal`, if there is one
      `value(t"North")` looks a case up by its exact name, and `value(Prim)` by its position;
      each yields `Unset` where nothing corresponds.
  entry #index   a case's zero-based ordinal, as an `Int`

entry .Identifiable   a two-way mapping between a naming convention and the names of a type's members
  detail
      An `Identifiable` is a pair of text functions: `#encode` converts a Scala name into the
      convention, and `#decode` converts back. The `Decodable.enumeration` decoder decodes
      through it before looking up a case, and reports valid names encoded through it, so a
      format can read `first_name` as `firstName`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Column()

      val snakeCase = Identifiable[Column](_.uncamel.snake, _.cut(t"_").camel)
      ```

      It is a `Typeclass.Pure`, so an instance captures nothing and may be shared freely.
  entry .apply   an `Identifiable` from an encoding function and a decoding function
  entry #encode   converts a Scala name into the convention
  entry #decode   converts a name in the convention back into a Scala name

entry .Parsable   the streaming counterpart of `Decodable`, reading `Self` directly from a format's token reader
  detail
      Where a `Decodable` receives an already-built syntax tree (a `Json`, an `Xml`), a
      `Parsable` drives the format's token reader over the input, so a composite value is
      built with no intermediate tree: no allocation of one, no boxing, no second traversal.
      `Transport` names the format (`Person is Parsable over Json`), and each format's subtrait
      fixes the `#Reader` (Jacinta's `Json.Parsable` sets it to `Json.Reader`).

      The reader is an exclusive, stateful capability lent for the duration of `#parse`;
      nothing of it may be retained in the result. Errors flow through the reader, which
      carries its own tactic, or through a tactic the instance captured when it was resolved,
      so `#parse` itself declares no error. Users rarely write one by hand: each format derives
      them (`derives Json.Direct`), and the `codecs` package chooses when they are used.
  refer distillate.Decodable
  refer distillate.Codecs
  entry #Reader   the format's token-reader capability, fixed by each format's subtrait
  entry #parse   reads a value of `Self` from the reader

entry .Number   the home of `Number.Error`, raised when text cannot be decoded as a number
  detail
      An object with no members but its error type.
  entry .Error   text which could not be decoded as a number, or whose number was out of range
    detail
        Error 405, with a subcode for its `#reason`: _`text` is not a valid `specializable`
        because `reason`_. Raised by the number decoders in the `Decodable` companion.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        capture[Number.Error](t"300".as[Byte]).reason   // Number.Error.Reason.OutOfRange
        capture[Number.Error](t"abc".as[Byte]).reason   // Number.Error.Reason.Unparseable
        ```
    entry .Reason   why the number could not be decoded: `Unparseable` or `OutOfRange`
      detail
          An enum of two cases: `Unparseable` (subcode 1), for text which is not a number at
          all, and `OutOfRange` (subcode 2), for a number the target type cannot represent.
      entry #number   the subcode the reason contributes to the error's number
    entry .communicable   renders a `Number.Error.Reason` as the clause of the error message
    entry #text   the text which could not be decoded
    entry #specializable   the numeric type the text was decoded as
    entry #reason   why it could not be decoded, as a `Number.Error.Reason`

entry .Truth   a spelling of truth and falsity, accepted by the `Boolean` decoder when in scope
  detail
      A `Truth` is a pair of words, `#affirmative` and `#negative`. The `Boolean` decoder
      collects every `Truth` in scope and accepts any of their spellings, after trimming and
      lowercasing the text, so several may be in scope together. `.trueFalse`, in this
      companion, is always found; the `truths` package adds `yes`/`no`, `on`/`off` and `1`/`0`
      by import, beside it rather than instead of it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      given Truth = Truth(t"ja", t"nein")

      t"Ja".as[Boolean]    // true
      ```
  refer distillate.truths
  refer distillate.Decodable.boolean
  entry .trueFalse   the spelling `true`/`false`, always in scope through the companion
  entry .Error   text which no `Truth` in scope recognizes as a `Boolean`
    detail
        Error 406: _`text` is not a recognized Boolean_. Raised by the `Boolean` decoder.
    entry #text   the text, as it was given
  entry #affirmative   the word for `true`, in lower case
  entry #negative   the word for `false`, in lower case
  entry #apply   reads text as `true` or `false` if it is one of the two words, or yields `Unset`
      Compares exactly: the `Boolean` decoder trims and lowercases before calling it.

entry .truths   further spellings of a `Boolean`, each accepted beside `true`/`false` when imported
  detail
      A choice package whose givens are imported by name, since `import truths.*` does not
      import givens. Unlike most choice packages, importing is additive: the `Boolean` decoder
      collects every `Truth` in scope, so each import adds a spelling.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import truths.{onOffTruth, oneZeroTruth}

      List(t"on", t"OFF", t"1", t"0").map(_.as[Boolean])   // List(true, false, true, false)
      ```
  refer distillate.Truth
  entry .yesNoTruth   accepts `yes` and `no` as a `Boolean`
  entry .onOffTruth   accepts `on` and `off` as a `Boolean`
  entry .oneZeroTruth   accepts `1` and `0` as a `Boolean`

entry .Codecs   the contextual mode which chooses between a format's derived and direct codecs
  detail
      Every format with generated ("direct") codecs offers two paths to a typed value: the
      derived one, which builds the format's syntax tree and decodes it (or encodes to one),
      and the direct one, which drives the format's token reader or printer straight into or
      out of the value. A format's companion contributes givens conditional on one of these
      modes, so a single import from the `codecs` package selects the path for every format at
      once. With none in scope, a type takes the direct path exactly when it has a direct
      instance of its own (an explicit given, or `derives`), and the derived path otherwise.

      Results are the same on either path; only the speed and the positions in error
      diagnostics differ.
  refer distillate.codecs
  refer distillate.Parsable
  entry .Direct   the mode taking a generated codec wherever a type is inlinable
  entry .Derived   the mode taking the derived codec, even over a direct instance
  entry .Strict   the mode taking generated codecs, and a compile error where a type has none
      A `Strict` is a `Direct`, so a format's generated instances apply under both; the
      strictness is added at the entry points (`read[Foo in F]`, `in[Text over F]`, `in[Data
      over F]`), which a format gates with `.strictFailure`.
  entry .strictFailure   the compile error a format reports under `codecs.strict` for a type with no direct codec
      A macro which halts compilation with _`format`: `T` has no direct codec, and
      `codecs.strict` does not take the derived one_. It is raised from within a given after
      that given has been selected, so it is the outcome of the search rather than a failed
      candidate which the search could pass over. For format authors.

entry .codecs   the three codec modes, each selecting a path for every format at once by import
  detail
      A choice package whose givens are imported by name, since `import codecs.*` does not
      import givens. The modes are alternatives: importing two at once makes their givens
      ambiguous wherever both would apply.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codecs.direct     // generated codecs wherever a type is inlinable
      ```

      `import codecs.derived` takes the derived codecs even over a direct instance, and `import
      codecs.strict` takes the generated codecs with a compile error, naming the type, wherever
      the derived path would otherwise be taken silently.
  refer distillate.Codecs
  entry .direct   selects generated codecs wherever a type is inlinable, for every format
  entry .derived   selects the derived codecs, even where a direct instance is in scope
  entry .strict   selects generated codecs, and makes a type with none a compile error

entry .internal   the macro implementations behind `Enumerable.derived` and `Codecs.strictFailure`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use.
  entry .strictFailure   the macro behind `Codecs.strictFailure`
  entry .enumerable   the macro behind `Enumerable.derived`
